{
 "id": "2023-chen-autumn",
 "name": "Autumn Colour in Urban Forests: Aesthetic Rating",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the aesthetic rating task in Chen, Huang, Shen, Fu, Yao, Huang, Lan, Zhu & Dong (2023), \"How Vegetation Colorization Design Affects Urban Forest Aesthetic Preference and Visual Attention: An Eye-Tracking Study\", Forests 14(7):1491, doi:10.3390/f14071491. The images are the authors' own photomontages of the Yushan Scenic Area in Fuzhou, China, printed in Figures 2 and 3 of the open-access paper (CC BY 4.0). They are used with credit for research, and the template is taken down if the authors object.\n\nWhat participants do: after an instruction quoted from the paper, each participant is randomly assigned to one of three image groups (10 images each) and sees the images in random order. For each image, a fixation cross appears for 3 s, the image is shown for 15 s, and then the participant rates its aesthetic quality on a 7-point scale. The page ends with gender and age.\n\nImages (30): canopy_O01–O12 are a UAV view over the forest canopy with autumn-coloured trees added in three forms (O1–O4 aggregate, O5–O8 homogeneous, O9–O12 random) and four intensities within each form (slight, low, medium and high, i.e. 15%, 22.5%, 33.75% and 50.63% coloured vegetation). forest_I01–I18 are nine in-forest scenes; each odd number is the original photo and the next even number is the same scene with colorized vegetation.\n\nOriginal study: 183 students of Fujian Agriculture and Forestry University in three groups of 61. Lab session with eye tracking. Analysis: mean rating per image, comparison of colorization intensities and forms against the original scene, and the marginal effect of intensity × form. Main finding: a homogeneous form and a moderate intensity gave the highest aesthetic ratings.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) 30 of the 31 images; the original base canopy photo is not printed in the paper. (2) The paper divided the images into three groups at random but does not list them; this template uses a fixed random split with 4 canopy and 6 forest images per group (listed in the image_group question). (3) No eye tracking; online on any screen instead of a lab monitor at 55 cm with a chin rest. (4) The paper gave a 5 s rating window after viewing; here the rating has no time limit. (5) The scale anchors are not reported in the paper; \"1 = very low\" and \"7 = very high\" were added. (6) Gender and age are asked at the end; the paper reports them but does not print its questions.",
